Kevin Wayne wrote:
> Quoth Boudewijn Waijers in the annals of <home.nl> ...
>> Kevin Wayne wrote:

> And there is, I'm sure, a broad middle category of player who doesn't
> want wizmode, but has a pet exploit or two that he doesn't want to see
> eliminated. What is an exploit? To my mind, it is something that is 1)
> unrealistic, even in terms of the game's milieu, and 2) profits the
> character who uses it in a way that minimizes or eliminates the
> obstacles placed in the player's path.

I agree with almost everything in your post. I think, just as you do,
that credit cloning should disappear in its current form, that infinite
pudding farming should have a cap, etc.

However, I think that a long mid-game does not fit the first of the two
criteria you mention: it's *not* unrealistic, neither in the game's
milieu, nor in real life to be self-supporting and slowly accumulating
riches. It does in a way minimise obstacles, so I grant you it fits your
second criterion in a way.

Hence, since it does not fit the first criterion, it isn't an exploit,
according to your definition.

> Possible future loopholes that may be closed could be shopkeepers
> granting credit for gold on the floor without even bothering to pick
> it up, or the death drop. Or maybe these will not be closed; maybe
> the DevTeam considers them features and will purposely leave them
> open. That's their decision.

I certainly hope they will. I don't pretend to know what the Dev Team
considers errors, abuses, or loopholes, and I guess they don't all agree
on this amongst themselves, but I do hope that they will close those
that they consider to be either.

> Presumably, there will always be players who think that aspects of
> the game need to be tightened up, and others who will object, "No!
> That's my favorite playing style! It's not fair to eliminate it.
> You're imposing a playing style on me that I don't want." The second
> group of people need to remember that it's the business of developing
> a game to create obstacles and challenges to the player. For most of
> us, the enjoyment of being a player depends on it.

Mostly agreed. However, I fail to see how keeping the possibility of a
prolonged mid-game for other players than yourself might lessen the
enjoyment of your own games.

Each to his own, I'd say.

